# Bundling the Loom CLI

Vite bundles the CLI program, the language server's TypeScript source, and Effect into a single `dist/main.js` module and adds a Node shebang, the line that makes the file directly executable. The installed `loom` binary therefore carries no runtime dependencies and no TypeScript source to compile.

## Vite build {Tangle}

The `node:` built-in modules stay external, since the runtime supplies them and the bundle need not carry them. Everything else — the language server's TypeScript source and Effect — is inlined into `dist/main.js`.

Design stays external too, for a different reason. They serve the overlay as a file read from their own package, resolved against the module doing the reading, so a copy inlined here would look for that file beside the CLI and never find it. Loom Design therefore loads Design where they are installed rather than carrying a copy, and `loom design` reports plainly when they are not there.

The language server's `exports` field points to TypeScript source files rather than pre-compiled output. Bun runs these files directly, as do tools like `tsx` and code editors, with no compilation step. Vite inlines them at build time, so the published CLI is pure JavaScript. The `loom lsp` command pulls in the server, and with it Volar; some of Volar's dependencies — the `vscode-*-languageservice` packages and `jsonc-parser` — ship only UMD bundles the bundler cannot follow statically, so the `umd2esm` plugin rewrites each to its ESM sibling, the same trick the language-server build uses.

`@athrio/loom-config` reads the configuration with the `yaml` parser, whose CommonJS build calls `require('process')`. An ES-module bundle exposes no `require`, so that call would throw at load time. The config resolves `yaml` to its ES-module build instead, which uses imports throughout and needs no `require`.

`ProseLanguage` loads `volar-service-markdown`, whose `vscode-markdown-languageservice` dependency imports `vscode-uri` by its default binding. The `vscode-uri` ES-module build exports no default, so the bundle resolves `vscode-uri` to its UMD build instead, where the bundler's CommonJS interop supplies one.

=>

import { defineConfig } from 'vite'
import { copyFileSync, existsSync } from 'node:fs'
import { builtinModules, createRequire } from 'node:module'
import { dirname, resolve } from 'node:path'
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url'

const here = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))
const requireFromHere = createRequire(import.meta.url)

const nodeBuiltins = [
  ...builtinModules,
  ...builtinModules.map((m) => `node:${m}`),
]

const umd2esm = {
  name: 'umd2esm',
  enforce: 'pre' as const,
  resolveId(source: string, importer: string | undefined) {
    if (/^(vscode-.*-languageservice|jsonc-parser)/.test(source)) {
      const fromDir = importer ? dirname(importer) : here
      const resolved = requireFromHere.resolve(source, { paths: [fromDir] })
      return resolved.replace(/\/umd\//, '/esm/').replace(/\\umd\\/g, '\\esm\\')
    }
    return null
  },
}

~

Design's browser assets travel with the CLI as files rather than as code. The overlay, the shell, and the setup screen are scripts a browser loads and pages it renders, so nothing in the command-line program imports them — Design names each one by path when a request for it arrives. A bundler follows imports, so it never learns these exist, and this plugin copies them beside `dist/main.js` after the bundle is written.

The list is written by hand, and that is where it fails. An asset added to Design and forgotten here breaks in a browser rather than in the build, because nothing compares this list against the paths Design asks for at runtime. The check below catches only the opposite mistake — a file named here that Design has not built stops the build and says which one.

=>

const designAssets = {
  name: 'design-assets',
  closeBundle() {
    const design = resolve(here, '..', 'loom-design')
    const carried: ReadonlyArray<readonly [string, string]> = [
      [resolve(design, 'dist', 'overlay.js'), 'overlay.js'],
      [resolve(design, 'dist', 'shell.js'), 'shell.js'],
      [resolve(design, 'dist', 'ui.js'), 'ui.js'],
      [resolve(design, 'src', 'shell.html'), 'shell.html'],
      [resolve(design, 'src', 'shell.css'), 'shell.css'],
      [resolve(design, 'src', 'ui.html'), 'ui.html'],
    ]
    for (const [from, name] of carried) {
      if (!existsSync(from)) {
        throw new Error(
          `the CLI carries Design's ${name}, and ${from} is not built — run the Design build first`,
        )
      }
      copyFileSync(from, resolve(here, 'dist', name))
    }
  },
}

~

Vite's library mode builds a single entry point to `dist/main.js`. The bundle targets Node 20, so Node runs it directly and Bun, being Node-compatible, runs the same bundle unchanged. The CLI package uses the ESM format — ECMAScript modules — so Vite emits an ESM bundle with a `#!/usr/bin/env node` shebang, making it directly executable. The VS Code extension runs in the editor's CommonJS host, so its Vite config emits that format instead.

=>

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [umd2esm, designAssets],
  resolve: {
    conditions: ['node'],
    mainFields: ['main', 'module'],
    alias: {
      yaml: resolve(
        dirname(requireFromHere.resolve('yaml/package.json')),
        'browser/index.js',
      ),
      'vscode-uri': requireFromHere
        .resolve('vscode-uri')
        .replace(/esm[\\/]index\.mjs$/, 'umd/index.js'),
    },
  },
  build: {
    target: 'node20',
    outDir: 'dist',
    emptyOutDir: true,
    sourcemap: true,
    minify: false,
    lib: {
      entry: resolve(here, 'src/main.ts'),
      formats: ['es'],
      fileName: () => 'main.js',
    },
    rollupOptions: {
      external: [...nodeBuiltins, 'bun:sqlite', 'node:sqlite'],
      output: { banner: '#!/usr/bin/env node' },
    },
  },
})
